<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>$(DEVELOPMENT_LANGUAGE)</string>
	<!-- The same two languages the macOS app declares, and for the same
	     reason: the catalogs live in the RelayiumAppKit package bundle rather
	     than in this one, so without this list iOS treats the app as
	     English-only. The list is what iOS decides the app is a candidate for,
	     and it is also what UIApplication.userInterfaceLayoutDirection — and so
	     SwiftUI's \.layoutDirection — is resolved from, which is why the APP
	     bundle needs its own copy of a list the package already ships.

	     Exactly the shipped set, in both directions. A language declared here
	     with no catalog behind it tells the device Relayium speaks it and then
	     renders English; the seven frozen locales now live in
	     apps/RelayiumKit/LocalizationArchive/ and must not reappear here. Keep
	     in step with AppLanguage; LocalizationIntegrityTests asserts the two
	     agree. -->
	<key>CFBundleLocalizations</key>
	<array>
		<string>en</string>
		<string>zh-Hans</string>
	</array>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
	<string>public.app-category.utilities</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- The two keys that make the app-owned Documents folder a real place the
	     user can reach. Received transfers land in Documents/Received, and
	     these publish that directory as "On My iPhone ▸ Relayium" in the Files
	     app: browsable, movable, deletable without this app. Without them the
	     files would exist inside a container nothing else can open, which for
	     a receive app is the same as not having saved them. -->
	<key>UIFileSharingEnabled</key>
	<true/>
	<key>LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- The one purpose string this app owes, and the reason it is owed is the
	     TRANSFER rather than the roster.

	     Two different things wear the word "nearby" here, and only one of them
	     touches the local network:

	      1. **Finding the other device is a server question.** `LanDiscoveryModel`
	         joins Relayium's code-less rendezvous room over the same HTTPS/
	         WebSocket origin as the rest of the app, and the server groups that
	         room by the public address it observes each device arriving from.
	         There is no Bonjour, no mDNS query and no subnet scan, which is why
	         `NSBonjourServices` and the multicast entitlement stay absent —
	         declaring either would ask for machinery this app genuinely does not
	         use.
	      2. **Carrying the bytes is not.** Once the user picks a device, every
	         realtime lane builds its peer connection with `iceTransportPolicy =
	         .all` (`RealtimeConnectionFactory.liveConnection`,
	         `LinkWorkspaceModel`), so the winning candidate pair is routinely a
	         unicast socket to the peer's address on this very subnet. iOS 14 and
	         later gate exactly that behind Local Network access, and iOS grants
	         it only to an app that has declared why it wants it.

	     Which makes the absence of this key a silent product failure rather than
	     a tidy minimalism. Retained physical runs `0af36138` and `56e78dbf`
	     recorded both halves of the shape: on iOS/iPadOS 26 the system withheld
	     the prompt altogether and the local path simply never connected, while
	     iPadOS 18 masked the omission and made the build look correct. A missing
	     purpose string is therefore not "one fewer permission"; it is a feature
	     that fails without ever asking.

	     This value is the ENGLISH FALLBACK. What a person actually reads comes
	     from `en.lproj/InfoPlist.strings` and `zh-Hans.lproj/InfoPlist.strings`
	     in this same bundle, which iOS resolves against the user's language
	     before it draws the alert; `IOSLocalNetworkPermissionTests` asserts the
	     English file and this string are the same sentence, so the fallback can
	     never drift into saying something else.

	     The sentence itself describes what the user is agreeing to and nothing
	     more: files and messages going straight to the device they picked. It
	     does not say Relayium looks at, scans or lists the network, because it
	     does not — see (1) — and a purpose string that overstates its reach is
	     the failure mode Apple's guidance is about.

	     Still not here, and deliberately: `NSCameraUsageDescription`. The camera
	     symbols the embedded WebRTC framework carries are an open, separately
	     scoped upload blocker recorded in `docs/ios-app-store-submission.md`;
	     inventing a purpose string for a feature this product does not have
	     would be a false statement to Apple and to the user. -->
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Relayium sends the files and messages you choose directly to the device you select, when that device is on the same network as this one.</string>
	<!-- Present and empty on purpose: an iOS app without a launch-screen
	     declaration is letterboxed at a legacy size rather than run at the
	     device's native resolution. Empty means the system's default, which is
	     what a single-screen app that has no launch imagery wants. -->
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict/>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
